WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.590 --> 00:00:06.200
On October 19, 2017

00:00:06.200 --> 00:00:08.760
at the Haleakala Observatory in Hawaii,

00:00:08.760 --> 00:00:10.860
human beings detected the first-ever

00:00:10.860 --> 00:00:13.650
visitor from interstellar space:

00:00:13.650 --> 00:00:16.843
an object from outside
our own solar system.

00:00:18.178 --> 00:00:20.660
A pair of Harvard
scientists say a massive,

00:00:20.660 --> 00:00:22.990
fast-moving visitor to our solar system

00:00:22.990 --> 00:00:24.300
may have been a probe

00:00:24.300 --> 00:00:26.860
sent by an advanced alien civilization.

00:00:26.860 --> 00:00:28.756
It's an alien spacecraft.

00:00:28.756 --> 00:00:30.550
Some sort of alien technology,

00:00:30.550 --> 00:00:32.313
perhaps exploring the cosmos.

00:00:33.470 --> 00:00:35.750
It's called Oumuamua,

00:00:35.750 --> 00:00:38.270
which roughly translates
as messenger from the past

00:00:38.270 --> 00:00:40.470
reaching out to us in Hawaiian.

00:00:40.470 --> 00:00:43.980
Everything in our solar
system is on a closed loop.

00:00:43.980 --> 00:00:45.930
You know, some orbits are nearly circular,

00:00:45.930 --> 00:00:47.510
some are elongated.

00:00:47.510 --> 00:00:49.390
This one was an open-ended orbit,

00:00:49.390 --> 00:00:52.303
which means it's coming from
outside the solar system.

00:00:54.740 --> 00:00:57.970
Then something even more strange happened.

00:00:57.970 --> 00:01:00.430
Upon leaving the inner solar system,

00:01:00.430 --> 00:01:03.720
astronomers reported an
unexpected acceleration,

00:01:03.720 --> 00:01:06.300
only adding to the
hypothesis that Oumuamua

00:01:06.300 --> 00:01:08.163
was some sort of alien creation.

00:01:10.730 --> 00:01:14.840
It would be exciting to think
this is an alien spacecraft.

00:01:14.840 --> 00:01:19.450
You know, one of the burning
questions on many minds is:

00:01:19.450 --> 00:01:20.980
Are we alone?

00:01:20.980 --> 00:01:22.690
But you have to be responsible about it.

00:01:22.690 --> 00:01:25.760
And if there is a perfectly
common explanation,

00:01:25.760 --> 00:01:28.453
you shouldn't go to
the exotic explanation.

00:01:30.830 --> 00:01:32.590
After observing the object,

00:01:32.590 --> 00:01:35.590
most astronomers agree
that it's probably a comet.

00:01:35.590 --> 00:01:37.300
Like 2I/Borisov,

00:01:37.300 --> 00:01:39.700
the other interstellar
object seen a year later.

00:01:41.160 --> 00:01:42.793
But we may never know for sure.

00:01:44.170 --> 00:01:45.690
Things that come in on these type

00:01:45.690 --> 00:01:49.240
of very elongated orbits move really fast

00:01:49.240 --> 00:01:50.630
when they're close to the sun,

00:01:50.630 --> 00:01:52.820
and then they're much
slower as they move out

00:01:52.820 --> 00:01:54.350
of the solar system.

00:01:54.350 --> 00:01:57.020
So when you looked at the
brightness of this object,

00:01:57.020 --> 00:02:00.653
it was going to only be easy
to observe for about a week.

00:02:02.850 --> 00:02:04.500
In that short window of time

00:02:04.500 --> 00:02:06.460
with just a pinpoint of light,

00:02:06.460 --> 00:02:09.520
scientists were able to make
guesses on the object's shape,

00:02:09.520 --> 00:02:13.010
its size, how fast it was
spinning and its color,

00:02:13.010 --> 00:02:15.090
which is thought to be reddish.

00:02:15.090 --> 00:02:17.500
But there are a lot of
questions about Oumuamua

00:02:17.500 --> 00:02:19.950
and space that'll remain unanswered

00:02:19.950 --> 00:02:21.853
unless we're able to act more quickly.

00:02:24.030 --> 00:02:27.310
Right now, many of the teams are saying

00:02:27.310 --> 00:02:29.400
we need the capability

00:02:29.400 --> 00:02:31.690
to have reactive missions,

00:02:31.690 --> 00:02:35.730
fast missions that can
respond to a discovery.

00:02:35.730 --> 00:02:37.650
Because right now, the missions,

00:02:37.650 --> 00:02:40.110
the way NASA works with
the small missions,

00:02:40.110 --> 00:02:42.780
is you put in a proposal
and it takes many years

00:02:42.780 --> 00:02:44.350
to get that approved.

00:02:44.350 --> 00:02:47.710
And then it might be a
decade between proposal

00:02:47.710 --> 00:02:49.570
and actually launching a mission.

00:02:49.570 --> 00:02:51.320
And obviously for Oumuamua,

00:02:51.320 --> 00:02:53.053
that would have been pointless.

00:02:54.040 --> 00:02:57.900
So we would like to have
missions that are maybe ready

00:02:57.900 --> 00:02:59.730
to go, built and in orbit,

00:02:59.730 --> 00:03:01.620
waiting for a new discovery

00:03:01.620 --> 00:03:04.020
or something that could
be launched immediately.

00:03:06.340 --> 00:03:07.930
That's why teams of scientists

00:03:07.930 --> 00:03:10.640
are planning missions to
wait for objects in space,

00:03:10.640 --> 00:03:12.270
and spring into action as soon as

00:03:12.270 --> 00:03:14.750
something interesting is detected.

00:03:14.750 --> 00:03:17.640
It may be humanity's only
chance to see an object

00:03:17.640 --> 00:03:19.663
from another solar system up close.

00:03:27.160 --> 00:03:31.130
The space between solar systems is huge.

00:03:31.130 --> 00:03:33.560
Unless warp drives become a reality,

00:03:33.560 --> 00:03:36.160
traveling from one to
another any time soon is

00:03:36.160 --> 00:03:37.790
probably not possible.

00:03:37.790 --> 00:03:40.670
Well, I mean, I'm a "Star Trek" fan,

00:03:40.670 --> 00:03:43.900
and according to "Star Trek" in 2063,

00:03:43.900 --> 00:03:47.610
I think, Zefram Cochrane
invents the warp drive.

00:03:47.610 --> 00:03:49.413
Zefram Cochrane?

00:03:51.230 --> 00:03:53.020
Of Alpha Centuri?

00:03:53.020 --> 00:03:55.410
The discoverer of the space warp?

00:03:55.410 --> 00:03:56.800
That's right captain.

00:03:56.800 --> 00:03:59.670
So if that happens, definitely.

00:03:59.670 --> 00:04:01.800
If not, I think it's quite difficult

00:04:01.800 --> 00:04:04.873
to reach anything within my lifetime.

00:04:07.360 --> 00:04:10.270
Currently, the only
way that we have to see

00:04:10.270 --> 00:04:13.160
some of the material from
these other star systems

00:04:13.160 --> 00:04:17.563
is stuff that is in interstellar
space that gets to us.

00:04:19.310 --> 00:04:20.580
Although we've never gone near

00:04:20.580 --> 00:04:22.090
an interstellar object,

00:04:22.090 --> 00:04:23.970
we've learned a lot
from approaching objects

00:04:23.970 --> 00:04:25.523
from within our solar system.

00:04:28.560 --> 00:04:32.560
In 2014, the European Space
Agency's Rosetta probe

00:04:32.560 --> 00:04:34.803
rendezvoused with Comet 67P.

00:04:36.760 --> 00:04:39.070
Rosetta is the first mission

00:04:39.070 --> 00:04:43.380
that ever delivered a lander
to the surface of a comet.

00:04:43.380 --> 00:04:45.740
This had never been done before.

00:04:45.740 --> 00:04:48.210
It's the first mission
that went into orbit

00:04:48.210 --> 00:04:49.430
around a comet,

00:04:49.430 --> 00:04:53.880
and it accompanied it
for two years and a bit,

00:04:53.880 --> 00:04:55.380
from 2014 to 2016.

00:04:56.957 --> 00:05:00.640
And from this mission, the
European Space Agency made a lot

00:05:00.640 --> 00:05:02.253
of interesting observations.

00:05:03.610 --> 00:05:06.930
I think this shape was
one of the major surprises

00:05:06.930 --> 00:05:08.100
that we had at the beginning.

00:05:08.100 --> 00:05:10.230
No one really expected that.

00:05:10.230 --> 00:05:13.420
Another really big
discovery, I think, was that

00:05:13.420 --> 00:05:17.870
the water that came off Comet 67P

00:05:17.870 --> 00:05:21.220
is not that kind of water
that we have on Earth.

00:05:21.220 --> 00:05:22.980
And if it's not the same, you know that

00:05:22.980 --> 00:05:26.053
they're probably not from the same source.

00:05:27.170 --> 00:05:29.180
So I think this was quite unexpected

00:05:29.180 --> 00:05:30.900
because people really thought

00:05:30.900 --> 00:05:33.880
this comet could be a real possibility

00:05:33.880 --> 00:05:35.413
to bring water to Earth.

00:05:39.750 --> 00:05:41.570
Scientists are still looking at the data

00:05:41.570 --> 00:05:42.940
from the Rosetta mission

00:05:42.940 --> 00:05:45.763
and making exciting new
discoveries about 67P.

00:05:46.900 --> 00:05:48.650
And those discoveries in turn

00:05:48.650 --> 00:05:51.400
give us hints about the early
days of our solar system.

00:05:53.250 --> 00:05:56.390
I think comets are very interesting

00:05:56.390 --> 00:05:59.040
because they can actually
tell us where we come from.

00:06:00.350 --> 00:06:04.250
The comets are always thought as kind of

00:06:05.530 --> 00:06:08.180
leftovers from the solar-system formation.

00:06:09.880 --> 00:06:12.190
And if we explore comets,

00:06:12.190 --> 00:06:15.570
we can understand how
the solar system formed,

00:06:15.570 --> 00:06:18.720
how Earth formed and basically

00:06:18.720 --> 00:06:21.060
how humans in the end came to be

00:06:21.060 --> 00:06:23.993
because without Earth we wouldn't be here.

00:06:28.166 --> 00:06:30.620
While the Rosetta mission was a success,

00:06:30.620 --> 00:06:33.910
Comet 67P orbits the sun every seven years

00:06:33.910 --> 00:06:35.590
and is affected by the heat,

00:06:35.590 --> 00:06:38.743
losing gases and changing its
composition with each orbit.

00:06:42.320 --> 00:06:45.440
Scientists at ESA recognized
the need to study a comet

00:06:45.440 --> 00:06:47.010
with a pristine surface,

00:06:47.010 --> 00:06:48.850
one that's never been near the sun,

00:06:48.850 --> 00:06:50.360
and that's largely unchanged

00:06:50.360 --> 00:06:52.760
since the formation of our solar system.

00:06:52.760 --> 00:06:54.413
This is known as a new comet.

00:06:56.320 --> 00:06:58.350
They were talking about this problem

00:06:58.350 --> 00:07:02.420
of pristine surface
versus changed surface.

00:07:02.420 --> 00:07:05.847
And they said, "Well, you
know what we should do?

00:07:05.847 --> 00:07:09.630
We should try and get to
one of those new comets."

00:07:09.630 --> 00:07:12.930
And so the Comet
Interceptor idea was born.

00:07:12.930 --> 00:07:15.310
This is very different
from previous missions

00:07:15.310 --> 00:07:17.810
that we've done where
we've always gone to comets

00:07:17.810 --> 00:07:20.860
that go round and round
in the solar system.

00:07:20.860 --> 00:07:23.060
And the reason why we go to these is that

00:07:23.060 --> 00:07:24.210
we know where they are.

00:07:24.210 --> 00:07:26.550
We can observe them and then get

00:07:26.550 --> 00:07:28.183
their position very accurately.

00:07:30.870 --> 00:07:32.910
But Comet Interceptor
doesn't want to do that.

00:07:32.910 --> 00:07:35.880
Comet Interceptor wants
to go to a very new comet

00:07:37.070 --> 00:07:41.970
and observe it with three
different little spacecraft:

00:07:41.970 --> 00:07:44.410
one mother spacecraft, as we call it,

00:07:44.410 --> 00:07:46.443
and two sub-spacecraft.

00:07:47.340 --> 00:07:50.820
And we want to basically
observe everything we can.

00:07:50.820 --> 00:07:53.420
We'll take pictures of
the comet's nucleus,

00:07:53.420 --> 00:07:55.500
which is a little thing in the middle.

00:07:55.500 --> 00:07:57.800
We'll take pictures of
the gases around it,

00:07:57.800 --> 00:08:00.150
the dust that comes off
of it, the environment.

00:08:02.650 --> 00:08:03.610
Interstellar objects

00:08:03.610 --> 00:08:06.760
are not Comet Interceptor's
primary target.

00:08:06.760 --> 00:08:08.370
Since we've only ever detected two,

00:08:08.370 --> 00:08:11.410
we don't know when or if
we'll detect another one.

00:08:11.410 --> 00:08:13.070
This mission is mainly going after

00:08:13.070 --> 00:08:16.690
unknown new comets from beyond
Neptune in the Oort cloud.

00:08:16.690 --> 00:08:19.500
This is the spherical, outer
part of the solar system

00:08:19.500 --> 00:08:23.010
and is believed to contain
trillions of comets.

00:08:23.010 --> 00:08:26.570
The majority have never
entered the inner solar system.

00:08:26.570 --> 00:08:28.160
But every once in a while,

00:08:28.160 --> 00:08:30.680
one of these icy bodies
gets a gravitational bump

00:08:30.680 --> 00:08:32.490
from another object, flinging it

00:08:32.490 --> 00:08:33.890
into the inner solar system.

00:08:34.940 --> 00:08:38.430
And Comet Interceptor hopes
to be ready for the next one.

00:08:38.430 --> 00:08:42.300
And there is another very
interesting target family,

00:08:42.300 --> 00:08:45.230
let's say, which is interstellar objects.

00:08:45.230 --> 00:08:49.450
So if there is an interstellar object

00:08:49.450 --> 00:08:52.030
like Oumuamua or Borisov,

00:08:52.030 --> 00:08:54.320
that's obviously where we we will be going

00:08:54.320 --> 00:08:57.690
because that's by far the
most interesting thing

00:08:57.690 --> 00:08:59.080
that we can explore.

00:08:59.080 --> 00:09:01.560
And Comet Interceptor is the only mission

00:09:01.560 --> 00:09:03.690
that has the capability to explore

00:09:03.690 --> 00:09:05.090
such an interstellar object.

00:09:07.840 --> 00:09:09.390
But to catch up to a new comet

00:09:09.390 --> 00:09:12.600
from our solar system or
an interstellar object,

00:09:12.600 --> 00:09:14.360
they needed to come up with a way to deal

00:09:14.360 --> 00:09:15.710
with the short lead time,

00:09:15.710 --> 00:09:17.558
something that's never been done before.

00:09:19.580 --> 00:09:21.010
The problem is that

00:09:21.970 --> 00:09:25.350
these dynamically new
comets are usually detected

00:09:25.350 --> 00:09:29.580
just a couple months before
they get close to Earth.

00:09:29.580 --> 00:09:32.430
And a couple months as everyone involved

00:09:32.430 --> 00:09:34.050
in any space mission knows

00:09:34.050 --> 00:09:36.800
is not enough to build a spacecraft

00:09:36.800 --> 00:09:39.273
and go somewhere with it.

00:09:40.500 --> 00:09:45.486
So we're actually more looking
at a timeframe of five years

00:09:46.320 --> 00:09:50.694
for the mission, where we have
five years of waiting time

00:09:50.694 --> 00:09:54.900
and we can choose whenever
we detect a suitable comet.

00:09:54.900 --> 00:09:57.810
And we think that the chance of detecting

00:09:57.810 --> 00:10:00.330
a suitable, dynamically new comet

00:10:00.330 --> 00:10:04.933
that we can reach is over
90% over these five years.

00:10:06.360 --> 00:10:08.360
To be able to wait for
a comet out in space

00:10:08.360 --> 00:10:11.040
for up to five years,
they'll send a small probe

00:10:11.040 --> 00:10:13.270
to the Earth-Sun Lagrange point,

00:10:13.270 --> 00:10:14.780
a place where the gravitational pull

00:10:14.780 --> 00:10:16.980
from both bodies cancel each other out,

00:10:16.980 --> 00:10:18.473
creating a stable orbit.

00:10:20.490 --> 00:10:22.770
And when Comet Interceptor gets there,

00:10:22.770 --> 00:10:25.960
it will just wait for five years.

00:10:25.960 --> 00:10:29.510
And once we detect the comet we'll be

00:10:29.510 --> 00:10:33.180
setting off towards rendezvous point,

00:10:33.180 --> 00:10:36.223
and this will be done
with chemical propulsion.

00:10:37.660 --> 00:10:39.500
Data gathered by the interceptor

00:10:39.500 --> 00:10:41.710
could be compared to other comet missions,

00:10:41.710 --> 00:10:43.680
so scientists will be able
to predict the effects

00:10:43.680 --> 00:10:45.070
of the sun on comets,

00:10:45.070 --> 00:10:47.050
giving them clues as to what the formation

00:10:47.050 --> 00:10:48.910
of our solar system was like,

00:10:48.910 --> 00:10:51.643
and in turn teach us about
how Earth came to be.

00:10:53.080 --> 00:10:55.030
It is a very ambitious mission.

00:10:55.030 --> 00:10:57.640
That's, I think, why it's so exciting.

00:10:57.640 --> 00:11:00.010
I mean, if you just do
things that are easy,

00:11:00.010 --> 00:11:02.010
it's a bit boring.

00:11:02.010 --> 00:11:05.170
So this mission is very
difficult to pull off.

00:11:05.170 --> 00:11:07.250
And I think that

00:11:07.250 --> 00:11:11.360
the team here at ESA
has done a very good job

00:11:11.360 --> 00:11:14.780
in kind of reminding everyone

00:11:15.620 --> 00:11:18.180
of the constraints of the mission

00:11:18.180 --> 00:11:21.470
while still getting the
best science out of it

00:11:21.470 --> 00:11:23.193
that we can expect at the end.

00:11:27.460 --> 00:11:28.730
Although not as far along

00:11:28.730 --> 00:11:30.480
as ESA's Comet Interceptor,

00:11:30.480 --> 00:11:32.890
which is set to launch in 2029,

00:11:32.890 --> 00:11:36.110
there is a team at MIT in
its initial planning stages,

00:11:36.110 --> 00:11:37.770
designing a mission specifically

00:11:37.770 --> 00:11:39.358
targeting interstellar objects.

00:11:41.520 --> 00:11:43.770
We see interstellar objects

00:11:43.770 --> 00:11:46.300
as the new frontier in

00:11:46.300 --> 00:11:48.580
planetary sciences.

00:11:48.580 --> 00:11:50.220
And now that we detected them,

00:11:50.220 --> 00:11:52.240
it really opens up the door of like, oh,

00:11:52.240 --> 00:11:54.330
what new things can we discover?

00:11:54.330 --> 00:11:56.830
And how do these new discoveries frame

00:11:56.830 --> 00:11:59.610
our thinking about the
formation of this star system

00:11:59.610 --> 00:12:01.020
and other star systems?

00:12:01.020 --> 00:12:02.963
So it's really science in a nutshell.

00:12:04.540 --> 00:12:06.130
Their proposal is called

00:12:06.130 --> 00:12:08.410
Dynamic Orbital Slingshot for Rendezvous

00:12:08.410 --> 00:12:10.370
with Interstellar Objects.

00:12:10.370 --> 00:12:12.220
Much like the Comet Interceptor,

00:12:12.220 --> 00:12:14.530
it will wait in space
and spring into action

00:12:14.530 --> 00:12:16.330
at the right time.

00:12:16.330 --> 00:12:18.720
So the unique part about our concept

00:12:18.720 --> 00:12:21.040
is that using the solar sail,

00:12:21.040 --> 00:12:23.590
our payload remains stationary.

00:12:23.590 --> 00:12:26.400
We can completely cancel
out this force of gravity.

00:12:26.400 --> 00:12:27.390
Of course, the materials

00:12:27.390 --> 00:12:29.770
that are required for this are exotic.

00:12:29.770 --> 00:12:31.700
Some of the materials are
currently being developed

00:12:31.700 --> 00:12:33.010
at academic labs.

00:12:33.010 --> 00:12:35.730
So we think it's within
the realm of possibility

00:12:35.730 --> 00:12:38.550
for a future mission to
do something like this.

00:12:38.550 --> 00:12:40.240
We can lay in wait,

00:12:40.240 --> 00:12:44.400
watch our local space for
any interstellar objects

00:12:44.400 --> 00:12:46.290
that might be entering that space

00:12:46.290 --> 00:12:49.760
and then spring to action
and get a close-up view

00:12:49.760 --> 00:12:51.273
of that interstellar object.

00:12:52.490 --> 00:12:54.430
Once an object is detected,

00:12:54.430 --> 00:12:56.660
it would be allowed to leverage
the gravitational force

00:12:56.660 --> 00:12:59.550
of the sun in a free-fall trajectory

00:12:59.550 --> 00:13:02.333
towards the detected
object, matching its path.

00:13:05.430 --> 00:13:08.170
The team received phase-one
funding from NASA,

00:13:08.170 --> 00:13:09.690
which gives them nine months to come up

00:13:09.690 --> 00:13:12.563
with a solid plan before
applying for the next phases.

00:13:14.680 --> 00:13:16.490
We're optimistic about phase two

00:13:16.490 --> 00:13:18.880
and we hope to find to
eventually get to a phase three.

00:13:18.880 --> 00:13:20.060
And if we're able to get there,

00:13:20.060 --> 00:13:23.083
then this technology becomes
closer to becoming reality.

00:13:26.640 --> 00:13:27.670
And both of these missions

00:13:27.670 --> 00:13:28.970
are coming at a good time.

00:13:31.480 --> 00:13:34.310
Telescopes that are coming
online in the next few years

00:13:34.310 --> 00:13:36.750
will be able to detect
more pristine comets

00:13:36.750 --> 00:13:39.303
and interstellar objects than ever before.

00:13:40.770 --> 00:13:42.720
Large synoptic survey telescope,

00:13:42.720 --> 00:13:44.490
the Rubin Observatory,

00:13:44.490 --> 00:13:46.000
is being built in Chile

00:13:46.000 --> 00:13:48.130
and that's going to be much bigger.

00:13:48.130 --> 00:13:50.720
And scientists are predicting

00:13:50.720 --> 00:13:52.740
we might start to see one of these

00:13:52.740 --> 00:13:56.733
each year once that is built,
because it goes much fainter.

00:14:00.000 --> 00:14:02.813
Why is it important to
study objects like this?

00:14:03.870 --> 00:14:08.460
Well, I think Oumuamua and
other interstellar objects

00:14:08.460 --> 00:14:13.460
are really important because
they are the likely leftovers

00:14:13.720 --> 00:14:16.353
of building planets in
another solar system.

00:14:17.850 --> 00:14:20.170
And right now, we don't
have the sensitivity

00:14:20.170 --> 00:14:24.740
or resolution to really
watch that process unfold.

00:14:24.740 --> 00:14:27.210
You know, for example,
are all solar systems

00:14:27.210 --> 00:14:30.460
chemically the same? Is it
the same physical processes

00:14:30.460 --> 00:14:31.550
that build planets?

00:14:31.550 --> 00:14:35.410
'Cause this is important for
the question of, are we alone?

00:14:35.410 --> 00:14:38.270
And so some of the ways
to answer those questions

00:14:38.270 --> 00:14:40.580
require detailed

00:14:40.580 --> 00:14:43.290
chemical compositional makeup,

00:14:43.290 --> 00:14:44.963
which you can't do remotely.

00:14:47.520 --> 00:14:49.640
I think we should be interested
in interstellar objects

00:14:49.640 --> 00:14:51.630
because it really tells us something about

00:14:51.630 --> 00:14:53.380
our own history, whether or not

00:14:53.380 --> 00:14:55.900
our star system is unique

00:14:55.900 --> 00:14:59.560
or is our star system similar
to other star systems?

00:14:59.560 --> 00:15:02.190
And if we find out maybe some compositions

00:15:02.190 --> 00:15:04.780
of the material around other star systems,

00:15:04.780 --> 00:15:07.073
it could inform us what's out there.

00:15:08.350 --> 00:15:11.310
If we can explore an interstellar object,

00:15:11.310 --> 00:15:13.460
we can explore

00:15:13.460 --> 00:15:16.850
by proxy another solar system.

00:15:16.850 --> 00:15:19.060
And that's something really exciting

00:15:19.060 --> 00:15:23.800
because we know there's so
many other solar systems

00:15:23.800 --> 00:15:25.590
with exoplanets

00:15:25.590 --> 00:15:27.270
and with maybe

00:15:27.270 --> 00:15:30.203
even exoplanets that
are kind of like Earth.

00:15:31.320 --> 00:15:33.670
And understanding those systems better

00:15:33.670 --> 00:15:35.385
I think is really interesting.

